scope: |
  A typeclass with MORE THAN ONE type parameter never resolves. `headAndArg` splits
  `MonadError[Option, Unit]` into `("MonadError", "Option, Unit")` — it cuts at a nested `[` and
  never at a comma — so the instance table is keyed on the string `Option, Unit` and no receiver
  can ever match it.

  REDUCED, not guessed. Identical body, identical call site, one difference:

      given o1:  M1[Option]        with … extension … def he …   Some(4).he(…) -> Some(4)   WORKS
      given oue: ME[Option, Unit]  with … extension … def he …   Some(4).he(…) -> REFUSED

  Four steps got there, and three of them killed a hypothesis: the plain patterns `case Some(_)` and
  `case Some(v)` work; the SAME extension body at top level works; the receiver shape is irrelevant
  (a `val` bound to `None`, a `val` bound to `Some`, and the bare constructor `Some(42)` all fail
  identically). Only the arity of the trait's type parameters matters.

  THIS CORRECTS `v3-handleError-on-a-val-bound-None-matches-no-arm`, which I filed YESTERDAY with
  the wrong mechanism in its title. It is not about `val`, not about `None`, and not about
  `handleError` — that method was simply the only multi-param instance the corpus reaches. Same
  shape of error as `v3-multishot-handler-without-a-return-clause` earlier in this session: I named
  the symptom the diagnostic pointed at instead of reducing to the smallest difference.

  THE FIX: cut the type argument at the first TOP-LEVEL comma as well as at a nested `[`, so
  `MonadError[Option, Unit]` keys on `Option`. Depth-aware, because `Foo[Map[K, V], Int]` must key
  on `Map` and a naive `indexOf(',')` reads the map's own comma.

  MEASUREMENT: N with floors DIFF 3 / CRASH 9. Expect `std-monaderror` to move — it is the corpus
  case built on `MonadError[F, E]` — and nothing to regress, since a key that matched nothing before
  cannot have been dispatching anything.
  MEASURED: N 218/369 unchanged, DIFF 3 and CRASH 9 held. N-NEUTRAL ON ITS OWN and that is the
  predicted result, not a disappointment: the two cases it unblocks — `std-monaderror` and
  `std-index` — are now refused by exactly one remaining thing, `'raise' is provided by a given
  instance` at 21:9 and `'pure' …` at 23:9. That is the arm from
  `v3-given-instance-as-a-receiver-is-refused`, measured yesterday and deliberately NOT shipped
  because it took DIFF from 3 to 5.

  WIDENED 2026-08-15 TO LAND THAT ARM TOO, before the edit. The filed entry states the order —
  "land the second, then the first, then re-measure" — and this is that step. They are one
  deliverable rather than two claims: the arm alone turns two honest refusals into wrong answers,
  and this fix alone unblocks nothing, because each case needs both. Same file, same mechanism, one
  measurement.

  THE PREDICTION, written before the numbers: DIFF back to 3, CRASH 9, PASS above 218. If DIFF rises
  again the arm comes out again — the floor decided that once already and nothing about wanting the
  result changes it.
next: re-apply the arm from the entry, measure the pair, push both or neither
